    "message": {
     "content": "### 1. Assessment: Is this a good offer?\n\n* **Market Benchmark:** For a Google L6 Senior Product Manager in the Bay Area, a total annualized package of ~$552,000 (at flat 25% equity) sits around the **60th\u201370th percentile**. The equity grant of $1.08M over 4 years is solid for an initial offer.\n* **Personal Benchmark:** **It is essentially a lateral move.** \n  * Your current Meta IC6 package is ~$526,000. \n  * The offer puts you at $552,000 (a modest ~5% bump).\n  * Your base salary would actually **decrease** from $240,000 to $235,000.\n  * There is **no sign-on bonus** to compensate for transition risk or walking away from unvested Meta equity.\n\nBecause Google recruited you while you were happily employed, you hold significant leverage. **Do not accept this offer as-is.** You should **negotiate aggressively**; if Google is unwilling to meet your target, you can comfortably walk away.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Key Details & Nuances to Note\n\n#### A. Vesting Schedule Verification\nEnsure you verify the specific vesting schedule in the offer documentation:\n* Google traditionally utilizes a **front-loaded vesting schedule** for GSUs in the US (typically **38% Year 1, 32% Year 2, 20% Year 3, 10% Year 4** or **33% / 33% / 22% / 12%**).\n* If your $1.08M grant is front-loaded at 38/32/20/10, your Year 1 equity is **$410,400** (Year 1 TC = ~$692,400), but your Year 4 equity drops to **$108,000**.\n* If the grant is straight-line (25% per year), Year 1 is $270,000.\n\n#### B. The Recruiter's Refresh Math\nRecruiters often present \"stacked refreshes\" as compounding upside. In reality, stacked refreshes are primarily designed to prevent the sharp compensation cliff that occurs after Year 2 or Year 4 when your initial grant finishes or steps down. Refreshes are strictly performance- and company-dependent, so negotiation should focus primarily on **guaranteed Year 1 & Year 2 money** (Base, Sign-on, and Initial Equity).\n\n---\n\n### 3. What to Ask For (Negotiation Targets)\n\n| Component | Current Meta | Initial Google Offer | **Counter-Offer Target** | Rationale |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $240,000 | $235,000 | **$255,000 \u2013 $260,000** | Posted range goes up to $271,000. A lateral move should not involve a base pay cut. |\n| **Sign-on Bonus**| $0 | $0 | **$75,000 \u2013 $100,000** | Standard tool for Google L6 to bridge unvested equity and make the move compelling. |\n| **Equity (4-Yr)**| ~$1,000,000 | $1,080,000 | **$1,200,000 \u2013 $1,250,000** | Top of the L6 band ($300k\u2013$312k/yr annualized). |\n| **Bonus** | ~15% ($36k) | 20% target ($47k) | **20% target** (non-negotiable structure) | Standard Google bonus target for L6. |\n\n---\n\n### 4. Step-by-Step Negotiation Strategy\n\n1. **Express genuine enthusiasm for the team and scope:** Reiterate that you enjoyed meeting the team and are excited about the product challenges.\n2. **Anchor on your passive status:** Remind the recruiter that you are in a great position at Meta and need a clear financial reason to take on the risk of switching.\n3. **Point out the base pay discrepancy immediately:** Make it clear that taking a lower base salary is a non-starter.\n4. **Bundle your ask (Base + Sign-On + Equity):** Give them clear, actionable targets rather than negotiating piecemeal.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Sample Negotiation Script\n\n> *\"Thank you so much for putting this offer together. I really enjoyed meeting the team and am genuinely excited about the scope of the role.*\n>\n> *Because I wasn't actively looking and have strong momentum and unvested equity at Meta, making a move needs to make clear financial sense. Right now, the offer represents a slight drop in base salary ($235k vs. my current $240k) and a total year-one package that is essentially flat compared to where I am.*\n>\n> *If we can bridge the gap on the following points, I would be thrilled to sign immediately:*\n>\n> 1. * **Base Salary:** **$255,000**, which sits comfortably within your posted range and reflects my current level and experience.\n> 2. * **Sign-on Bonus:** A **$100,000** first-year sign-on bonus to offset the equity transition and unvested value I would be leaving behind.\n> 3. * **Equity:** Adjusting the new-hire GSU grant to **$1,250,000** over four years.\n>\n> *I want to make this work and hope we can reach a package that reflects the seniority of the role and makes the transition compelling.\"*",
